 {:db/id "q-106930" :question/id #uuid "15beea94-47dc-575a-be81-e2d3b2547e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106930" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106930/ma-source-field/63683f59ef36b06ee829c06b20181766c556038dae9a19fc64d4794864f4e801"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
