 {:db/id "q-106933" :question/id #uuid "17d56701-363e-5629-ba43-f0ff54124002" :question/math-academy-id "q-106933" :question/problem "Let $I$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)={2}^{ax},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$I$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, $f(x)={2}^{ax}>0$ for all $x\\in R$ for any $a\\in R,$ hence the zero function $z(x)=0\\notin I.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in I.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106933/ma-source-field/841c6c3432c6b46f5764a363e7054fc01bbbe051af8f425f64cf24ad22fbb77e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
