 {:db/id "q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733" :answer-field/id #uuid "224da74b-2945-552e-a5c2-10b9f76d854e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733/8548bd60179d299eff7d6747f28965504d3f34e77b39a1393c83c8f18ac5f2ff" "q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733/42425508260aaac0bb9b75c117f5aa70006afc090bdfbbaf7626176060efe58c" "q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733/18c8c2e802b239aec5e0068b977dd2e986ca2cb9cfb472d5337b8d531de74d73" "q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733/cd2f4f1ba4b85266177b8d2de99c4fd5e2a46a1fa3e4a1887198aa78a4362d72" "q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733/284e335524189faa6f766ad48fcb3df16b33ea88eeb30a8546fc0d07ba5a1687"] :answer-field/correct "q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733/284e335524189faa6f766ad48fcb3df16b33ea88eeb30a8546fc0d07ba5a1687" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-113918" :question/id #uuid "c2081a2b-f662-517d-bd66-e0ddf60008b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113918" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113918/ma-source-field/720717825c12e4ed3e6662ba6a7ebf4991022dba08ee332a513f78d45eaee733"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
