 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "097f3e11-2ce8-55f7-bbfa-1640ee282b68"] :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-47650/ma-source-field/755e5b752eb9d2d2f547b6466c9b0c447faec7cf0b4ebcd497ca12ec9db32558"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
