 {:db/id "q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203" :answer-field/id #uuid "689b4114-c1d3-5f0f-8182-74e6a0c999f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203/5b59075ed7ac6aa9dfffb13153b307cfacca4bd8854d0f374520f9e6db8d4f87" "q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203/c708ad0e8231b3433d9a634fbae6fe3ba27d073670da06247746a109ace66528" "q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203/0254c053a8fcf9e56483b2456fce38aaf17ebd8281a4ef72b0e22e947931a0b7" "q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203/4323da32f4d0e343524480efb3fc2e33f66f54e3b33c2db4aac6648792846d8c" "q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203/5d3f1a556d9a2e94091143fad5710d86f21160731421b4fb0b96c6ee297dba08"] :answer-field/correct "q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203/5d3f1a556d9a2e94091143fad5710d86f21160731421b4fb0b96c6ee297dba08" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-94798" :question/id #uuid "f0cbcdef-8d32-524c-bf12-b31a8feacf80" :question/math-academy-id "q-94798" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials in the real  variable $x$ with coefficients in $Q.$ Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed.** *\n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomial  $f(x)=x.$ Then $f\\in S$, but $\\sqrt{2}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ }x\\notin S$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- The zero function belongs to $S.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in S.$ $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94798/ma-source-field/599390c242536d25a8888ac11d0fd7bb38dd7a416c4d81b1e64ef82e92edf203"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
